  "lineage": "Tahitian himene rū'au (\"chant of the elders\") slow-tempo unaccompanied chant tradition of the Society Islands (French Polynesia), distinct from the better-known himene tarava festival-chorus form. Performance: elders sing slow-tempo Tahitian-language sacred-or-genealogical verse in unison or two-part configuration, with characteristic free-rhythm phrasing and chest-register projection. The form survived the post-1810 Christianization period as a sanctioned-folk-cultural practice and entered the twentieth-century Polynesian-cultural-revival as a marker of pre-contact tradition. The annual Heiva i Tahiti festival hosts himene competitions across multiple categories. Distinguished from himene tarava by himene rū'au's slow-tempo elder-led (vs festival-chorus mass-participation) functional context.",
